<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georgians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Georgians are a Kartvelian-speaking people of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> who form the majority of the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. Christian since the conversion of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in the 4th century, they have their own script and church, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a>. Divided for much of their history among rival kingdoms and principalities, they were united under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a>, most gloriously in the 12th and early 13th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georgians.jpg" alt="Georgians" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Wagner · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georgia,_Kutaisi,_Gelati_Monastery_(29812678274).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Georgians call themselves Kartveli, their land Sakartvelo and their language Kartuli. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> knew western Georgians as Colchians and eastern Georgians as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a>. Most historians agree that the ancestors of modern Georgians lived in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. Western tribes established the kingdom of Colchis, dated to about 1350 BCE, and eastern tribes formed their kingdom in the 7th century BCE. Between the 6th and 4th centuries BCE, Kartlian tribes consolidated around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a> and founded the Kingdom of Kartli, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, under Pharnavaz I. By the mid-4th century both Lazica and Iberia were officially Christian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> invaded south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 645. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a> rose in the 9th century, and Bagrat III, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-iii-kuropalates">David III of Tao</a>, was crowned first king of a unified Georgia in 1008. Under David IV (r. 1089–1125), who defeated Turkish forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-didgori">Battle of Didgori</a> in 1121, and Tamar (r. 1184–1213), Georgia reached its height, commonly called the Georgian Golden Age. Jalal ad-Din captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> in 1226, and the Mongol invasions followed. The kingdom collapsed into anarchy by 1466 and split into three kingdoms and five principalities.</p><p>In 1783 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-georgievsk">Treaty of Georgievsk</a> made eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> a Russian protectorate under Heraclius II. Russia annexed eastern Georgia in 1801, abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a> and ended the autocephaly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a>. Georgia declared the Democratic Republic of Georgia on 26 May 1918, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> occupied it in February 1921. The Supreme Council declared independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> on 9 April 1991.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Georgian is a Kartvelian language with no known relation to any other language family, with its own writing system and a written tradition dating back to the 5th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svans">Svans</a>, Mingrelians and the Laz speak related Kartvelian languages. The Church was crucial to the development of a written language, and most of the earliest works were religious texts. Most Georgians are Eastern Orthodox Christians belonging to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a>. There are about 5 million Georgians in the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georgians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1978 Armenians and <b>Georgians</b> defeated a proposal to remove their languages' official status.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Greeks dominated the city and the coastal towns, while Laz communities lived in the east and Armenians, <b>Georgians</b> and, increasingly, Turkmen pastoralists lived in the interior.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Georgians</b> trace their state to Colchis and Iberia; King Mirian III adopted Christianity in 337, Bagrat III was crowned King of Georgia in 1010, and the Golden Age of David IV and Queen Tamar (r. 1184–1213) left cathedrals and a national epic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/georgia">Georgia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…earliest Georgian formation', which with the Kingdom of Iberia contributed to the Kingdom of Georgia and the Georgian nation; Ronald Grigor Suny stresses the Colchians' part in the ethnogenesis of the <b>Georgians</b>, and Svante Cornell dates proto-Georgian statehood to the kingdom of Colchis in the 6th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Later <b>Georgians</b> looked back to the united kingdom as the model of statehood; David the Builder and Tamar were canonised by the Georgian Orthodox Church and remain central figures of national memory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Iberia, known to <b>Georgians</b> as Kartli, was the principal state of eastern Georgia in Antiquity, centred on the valley of the Mtkvari (Kura) river where it meets the Aragvi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
